# Template, don't echo

When the user pastes a concrete value into chat — an SSH host/port, an API key,
a connection string, an AWS account ID, an internal hostname, a personal email,
a vast.ai instance address, etc. — DO NOT copy the literal value into files I
write (scripts, READMEs, configs, comments, docs, examples). Always substitute
a placeholder.

Apply to: any file that will be committed to git, plus any file that lives in
the workspace and could be shared (READMEs, helper scripts, docs, example
configs, test fixtures). Local-only files explicitly listed in `.gitignore`
(`.env`, `secrets/`, etc.) are the only acceptable place for real values, and
even there, only if the user asked me to put them there.

## Substitution conventions

- SSH commands: `ssh -p PORT USER@HOST` (not `ssh -p 40230 root@1.2.3.4`)
- API keys: `${OPENAI_API_KEY}` or `sk-...REDACTED...`
- URLs: `https://HOST/path` or `https://example.com/path`
- IPs in docs: use RFC 5737 ranges (`192.0.2.0/24`, `198.51.100.0/24`,
  `203.0.113.0/24`) or `HOST`
- Account IDs / org IDs: `ACCOUNT_ID`, `ORG_ID`

## Concrete example of what NOT to do

The user once said `ssh -p 40230 root@1.2.3.4 -L 8080:localhost:8080`.
Wrong response: paste `40230` and `1.2.3.4` into a helper script's docstring
and a README example. Right response: write `PORT` and `HOST` and add a
one-line note explaining where the user should sub in their own values.

## Self-check before writing any file

Ask: "Does this file contain anything the user typed verbatim that identifies
a host, an account, a key, or a person?" If yes, template it. If unsure, ask.
